+          "claim": "Let $B_V:=\\{u\\in V:\\|u\\|_V\\le1\\}$. For any $\\varepsilon>0$, choose $N$ so $1/\\sqrt{\\lambda_{N+1}}<\\varepsilon/2$. If $x=Ju$ with $u\\in B_V$, then $\\sum_{j>N}|x_j|^2=\\sum_{j>N}|u_j|^2\\le\\lambda_{N+1}^{-1}\\sum_{j>N}\\lambda_j|u_j|^2\\le\\lambda_{N+1}^{-1}$. The set $C_N:=\\{(z_1,\\ldots,z_N)\\in\\mathbb K^N:\\sum_{j\\le N}\\lambda_j|z_j|^2\\le1\\}$ is closed and bounded in $\\mathbb R^N$ (if $\\mathbb K=\\mathbb R$) or $\\mathbb R^{2N}$ (if $\\mathbb K=\\mathbb C$), so it is compact by Heine--Borel and has a finite $\\varepsilon/2$-net with centers in $C_N$. Each center $z$ lifts by zero padding to $\\widetilde z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_N,0,\\ldots)\\in J(B_V)$, because its weighted energy is at most one. For $x=Ju\\in J(B_V)$, the prefix $(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$ belongs to $C_N$; the net and the tail bound therefore give $\\|x-\\widetilde z\\|_{\\ell^2}<\\varepsilon$ for one of these finitely many lifts. Thus $J(B_V)$ is totally bounded with centers in itself. Its closure $C:=\\overline{J(B_V)}$ is also totally bounded: a finite $\\varepsilon/2$-net for $J(B_V)$ is an $\\varepsilon$-net for $C$ by the triangle inequality and the definition of closure. The set $C$ is complete because it is closed in the complete Hilbert space $H$: every Cauchy sequence in $C$ converges in $H$, and closedness keeps its limit in $C$. By [F4], $C$ is compact. The unit-ball criterion in [[def-compact-linear-operator]] now shows that $J$ is compact.",
